\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Comparison with hierarchical renormalization}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{The zero-row-sum cancellation and the product-preserving
Gaussian block integral occur in
\citet{arXiv v2, Lemmas 5.1.3 and 5.1.6, pp. 67-69}{bauerschmidt2019renormalisation}.
The finite Wick calculation in \ref{fdip-01DG} uses their scalar
mechanism with arbitrary integer #{p\ge2}. In the book's notation,
#{p=L^d}, #{\lambda=L^{-2}}, and the present nonnegative
hierarchical Laplacian is #{-\Delta_{H,k}}. Their covariance and
the covariance here both use a Gaussian density with exponent
#{-\langle x,Qx\rangle /2}.}

\p{For their scalar polynomial
#{g_{\mathrm B}x^4/4+\nu_{\mathrm B}x^2/2}, the conversion from
#{gW_4(x;s)+\nu W_2(x;s)} is
##{g_{\mathrm B}=4g,\qquad
\nu_{\mathrm B}=2\nu -12sg,}
with additive constant #{3gs^2-\nu s}.
At #{p=L^4} and zero Gaussian mass, their coefficient
#{9(1-L^{-4})} converts to
#{\beta_p=36(1-1/p)}; see
\citet{arXiv v2, Proposition 5.3.1 and Lemma 5.3.4, pp. 78-80}{bauerschmidt2019renormalisation}.
For nonzero Gaussian mass, their scale-dependent coefficient has the
additional factor #{(1+m^2L^{2j})^{-2}}. This Gaussian mass is distinct
from the polynomial coefficient #{\nu_{\mathrm B}} above.
The mass conversion includes the Wick-variance shift.}

\p{The book's global control, including its tuned initial mass,
uses sufficiently large integer #{L}; see
\citet{arXiv v2, Theorem 8.2.4 and Section 8.3, pp. 120-125}{bauerschmidt2019renormalisation}.
That result is cited, not proved here, and does not supply the
all-integer-#{p} hypotheses of \ref{fdip-01DO}. Its remainder
is additive outside the exponential, whereas #{R} in
\ref{fdip-01DN} is part of the potential. The author corrections
of 22 October 2026 amend the cancellation discussion, mass
uniqueness and continuity, and the large-field estimate:
\citet{items 3, 16-17 and 25}{bauerschmidt2026renormalisationerrata}.
These corrected source results are comparisons; the estimates in
\ref{fdip-01DN} follow from its stated input bounds.}
